00:01:28.316 --> 00:01:31.775
Executive Director of the
Siegel Family Endowment.

00:01:31.775 --> 00:01:33.495
Good to see you, Katy.

00:01:33.495 --> 00:01:34.465
- It's good to see you too.

00:01:34.465 --> 00:01:35.558
Thanks for having me.

00:01:36.715 --> 00:01:39.025
- So maybe a good place to start is,

00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:41.482
what is a family endowment and what is

00:01:41.482 --> 00:01:44.325
the Siegel Family Endowment in particular?

00:01:44.325 --> 00:01:45.205
What's its focus?

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:46.038
What does it do?

00:01:46.038 --> 00:01:47.415
What's the work of it?

00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:48.845
- Yeah, that's a really good question.

00:01:48.845 --> 00:01:52.045
So endowment for us, for
some people this is not true,

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:55.375
but for us is really just
a fancy replacement word

00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:58.785
for foundation because someone
else had already established

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.412
a Siegel Family foundation.

00:02:00.412 --> 00:02:01.626
And so David, (indistinct).
- Oh really,

00:02:01.626 --> 00:02:03.275
I didn't know that.

00:02:03.275 --> 00:02:07.055
- The chairman and my boss (indistinct)

00:02:07.055 --> 00:02:09.645
and really we're a foundation,
a private foundation.

00:02:09.645 --> 00:02:11.345
So that means we give away money,

00:02:11.345 --> 00:02:13.655
but also engage in other activities.

00:02:13.655 --> 00:02:16.125
We support research, we support
really great non-profits

00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:19.235
like Khan Academy that are
doing really important work

00:02:19.235 --> 00:02:20.085
out in the world.

00:02:20.085 --> 00:02:23.225
But what we really try to do,
that's a little bit different

00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:26.065
because we're smaller foundation
and a little bit newer

00:02:26.065 --> 00:02:28.125
on the scene is also trying to think about

00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:29.925
how we can change conversations.

00:02:29.925 --> 00:02:33.065
How we can do some thought leadership

00:02:33.065 --> 00:02:37.725
and really are focused on
reframing questions and ideas.

00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:40.138
So not just writing a
check and sitting back

00:02:40.138 --> 00:02:43.005
and hoping for the best,
but really thinking

00:02:43.005 --> 00:02:45.165
about how we can help
change the conversation

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:47.485
and help people think about the impact

00:02:47.485 --> 00:02:49.460
of technology on society,
which is our focus,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.095
in a different way.

00:02:51.095 --> 00:02:53.822
And so, that is both the positive impact

00:02:53.822 --> 00:02:56.525
and the sometimes negative
impact of tech on society.

00:02:56.525 --> 00:02:59.055
And we're looking at
both sides of the coin

00:02:59.055 --> 00:03:01.655
and trying to figure out ways
to leverage tech for good

00:03:01.655 --> 00:03:04.915
and also to help mitigate
some of the downsides

00:03:04.915 --> 00:03:05.748
that have come.

00:03:06.805 --> 00:03:09.785
- Yeah, and as you mentioned,
the Siegel Family Endowment

00:03:09.785 --> 00:03:11.995
is a longtime major
funder of Khan Academy.

00:03:11.995 --> 00:03:14.845
So everyone watching who enjoys
Khan Academy's existence,

00:03:16.285 --> 00:03:20.575
please think Katy and David,
who's family endowment.

00:03:20.575 --> 00:03:22.085
This isn't, I'm not saying
this cause you're in the room.

00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:23.135
I would say this, even if you weren't

00:03:23.135 --> 00:03:27.175
in the video conference
or whatever we're doing.

00:03:27.175 --> 00:03:30.495
But maybe a good place
to is to double click

00:03:30.495 --> 00:03:33.215
on what you just said,
that your focus is on tech

00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:36.845
and it's mitigating, its
negative implications

00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:38.755
and promoting its positive.

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:39.955
What are some of those things?

00:03:39.955 --> 00:03:41.845
What are some of the things
that you y'all are worried about

00:03:41.845 --> 00:03:44.155
that you have to actively
make sure that tech

00:03:44.155 --> 00:03:46.525
doesn't go bad, so to speak.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.765
And then what are the things
that you could promote

00:03:48.765 --> 00:03:50.995
where the tech could be a force for good.

00:03:50.995 --> 00:03:54.075
- Yeah, so I think we've
seen a lot in terms

00:03:54.075 --> 00:03:57.379
of misinformation or
disinformation on the internet,

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:00.355
where tech and social media platforms,

00:04:00.355 --> 00:04:03.320
communication platforms
can be used for bad,

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.240
where you have micro-targeting,
where you can really

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.240
do things in the darkness,
the sort of underbelly of tech

00:04:11.295 --> 00:04:15.695
and the way that advertising
and conversation targeting

00:04:15.695 --> 00:04:19.245
can work is really something
that we've been looking at

00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:20.078
for a long time.

00:04:20.078 --> 00:04:22.495
And lots of people are looking
at this from different angles

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:25.705
from academic researchers to
institutions that are trying

00:04:25.705 --> 00:04:27.915
to combat and come up
with practical solutions

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:29.325
for this stuff.

00:04:29.325 --> 00:04:30.605
So that's just one example.

00:04:30.605 --> 00:04:33.448
I think there are other economic examples

00:04:33.448 --> 00:04:37.675
of how tech can and has accelerated some

00:04:37.675 --> 00:04:38.945
of the divide in the country.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.328
When you think about people
who are benefiting the most

00:04:41.328 --> 00:04:44.235
from tech companies and those who aren't.

00:04:44.235 --> 00:04:47.505
So we look at all of
those things holistically

00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:50.505
and think about how they
intersect and how we can try

00:04:50.505 --> 00:04:55.505
to help soften the steep differences

00:04:56.265 --> 00:04:59.235
and how we can help change
some of that for the better.

00:04:59.235 --> 00:05:01.155
I think the flip side of that,
where you can leverage tech

00:05:01.155 --> 00:05:03.135
for good is moments like
this, where we're able

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:06.615
to have these conversations,
share with people new ideas,

00:05:06.615 --> 00:05:09.305
new concepts, online, speak
to really broad audiences

00:05:09.305 --> 00:05:11.655
around the world, connect and share.

00:05:11.655 --> 00:05:14.245
Where you have things like
Khan, where people can learn

00:05:14.245 --> 00:05:17.045
outside of the traditional
classroom setting

00:05:17.045 --> 00:05:20.155
and really have access
to educational resources

00:05:20.155 --> 00:05:21.645
that are really powerful.

00:05:21.645 --> 00:05:24.295
And even the same communication
platforms I was talking

00:05:24.295 --> 00:05:26.160
about before that have been used for harm,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.135
have also done a lot of good for people

00:05:28.135 --> 00:05:30.785
who have found community
online where maybe

00:05:30.785 --> 00:05:32.285
they didn't have it in person.

00:05:33.235 --> 00:05:34.575
- Yeah, and I wanna dig more into that.

00:05:34.575 --> 00:05:36.255
I know you've just
published this white paper

00:05:36.255 --> 00:05:38.635
on how infrastructure
influences everything,

00:05:38.635 --> 00:05:41.795
but on that first part
around tech, I am fascinated.

00:05:41.795 --> 00:05:45.427
I don't think anyone has some
obvious good solutions here,

00:05:45.427 --> 00:05:49.155
but you mentioned the
micro-targeting and social media

00:05:49.155 --> 00:05:52.682
and the conversations
that I've been a part of,

00:05:52.682 --> 00:05:55.535
the social media and its impact
on especially young people

00:05:55.535 --> 00:05:56.625
and their self perception.

00:05:56.625 --> 00:05:58.275
And could it drive anxiety and depression,

00:05:58.275 --> 00:06:01.255
but there's also social
media for polarizing us

00:06:01.255 --> 00:06:04.235
where you only get what
you, and maybe you engage

00:06:04.235 --> 00:06:06.907
even deeper in something that
polarizes you and further.

00:06:06.907 --> 00:06:09.785
Do you all have any, are
y'all talking to some people

00:06:09.785 --> 00:06:11.515
that you think have good
solutions who can thread

00:06:11.515 --> 00:06:15.015
that needle between free
speech, but not speech

00:06:15.015 --> 00:06:18.158
that does ... it's a
really difficult question.

00:06:19.055 --> 00:06:20.635
- Yeah, it is a really difficult question.

00:06:20.635 --> 00:06:23.905
And I think, we have
friends and colleagues

00:06:23.905 --> 00:06:28.265
at research institutions like
the Stanford has a project

00:06:28.265 --> 00:06:30.905
in their philanthropy
and civil society center

00:06:30.905 --> 00:06:33.015
that's about digital civil society.

00:06:33.015 --> 00:06:35.635
And some of the researchers
who are associated with that

00:06:35.635 --> 00:06:37.115
are looking at these questions.

00:06:37.115 --> 00:06:40.085
I think there are no easy answers.

00:06:40.085 --> 00:06:42.725
One of the things that I
default to often that I think

00:06:42.725 --> 00:06:45.045
is really important to talk
about, is transparency.

00:06:45.045 --> 00:06:50.045
So I think human beings we're
smarter than maybe the tech

00:06:50.215 --> 00:06:52.765
and social media platforms
wanna give us credit for.

00:06:52.765 --> 00:06:57.325
And if you know that you're
being shown search results,

00:06:57.325 --> 00:07:01.045
for example, that are aligned
with one point of view

00:07:01.045 --> 00:07:04.005
that an algorithm thinks
is your point of view,

00:07:04.005 --> 00:07:07.075
or if you know that you're being targeted

00:07:07.075 --> 00:07:09.175
by a specific advertising.

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:11.455
I think that knowledge
can be really powerful

00:07:11.455 --> 00:07:14.125
in terms of helping you
understand the context

00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:16.185
around the information that you're seeing

00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:20.345
and helping you think
differently about whether or not

00:07:20.345 --> 00:07:22.935
it's true and verifiable.

00:07:22.935 --> 00:07:27.205
We have a really wide range
of resources on the internet

00:07:27.205 --> 00:07:30.515
and some of them are more
fact-checked than others.

00:07:30.515 --> 00:07:33.475
So I think that transparency
and context can do a lot

00:07:33.475 --> 00:07:36.285
to help combat some of that polarization

00:07:36.285 --> 00:07:37.545
misinformation space.

00:07:37.545 --> 00:07:39.695
And I think that's one of
the places where practical

00:07:39.695 --> 00:07:44.198
solutions are being experimented
with, really interestingly.

00:07:45.275 --> 00:07:46.108
- No, I really liked that.

00:07:46.108 --> 00:07:47.535
Actually I haven't heard that
before, but you're right.

00:07:47.535 --> 00:07:50.655
If at all you told me is
like, "Hey, because you are

00:07:50.655 --> 00:07:55.065
a middle-aged man, who's into
alien conspiracy theories.

00:07:55.065 --> 00:07:57.295
We are now going to give you this."

00:07:57.295 --> 00:08:00.857
I think that is my profile (laughs)

00:08:00.857 --> 00:08:03.845
- (indistinct) and we're
gonna dig into that.

00:08:03.845 --> 00:08:05.903
- Middle-aged man who likes to read about

00:08:05.903 --> 00:08:08.307
alien conspiracy theories.

00:08:08.307 --> 00:08:10.535
Therefore here's your top search results.

00:08:10.535 --> 00:08:12.975
And I'm like, wait, I'm
not a middle aged man.

00:08:12.975 --> 00:08:15.545
And no, I am. (laughs)

00:08:15.545 --> 00:08:17.095
I just turned 44 on Sunday.

00:08:17.095 --> 00:08:20.357
So I've come to terms with my--

00:08:20.357 --> 00:08:21.745
- Happy birthday.
- I still can't get over

00:08:21.745 --> 00:08:25.575
the fact that 44 plus 16 is 60.

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:26.995
I know I'm known for teaching math,

00:08:26.995 --> 00:08:29.385
but I keep thinking I'm
doing the math wrong,

00:08:29.385 --> 00:08:31.185
but anyway, back to the subject.

00:08:31.185 --> 00:08:34.745
So on this notion of
infrastructure obviously

00:08:34.745 --> 00:08:36.785
where's technical infrastructure
or digital infrastructure

00:08:36.785 --> 00:08:38.325
is a piece, and this is what you analyze

00:08:38.325 --> 00:08:39.675
in your white paper.

00:08:39.675 --> 00:08:42.695
But maybe the first question
is why did y'all decide

00:08:42.695 --> 00:08:46.495
to write a white paper on infrastructure

00:08:46.495 --> 00:08:48.565
and what were kind of the big ahas?

00:08:49.765 --> 00:08:53.715
- Yeah, so a few things I
will say that it started

00:08:53.715 --> 00:08:56.075
because I am an infrastructure nerd.

00:08:56.075 --> 00:08:57.775
David is an infrastructure nerd.

00:08:57.775 --> 00:09:00.845
We just have always
connected on questions of,

00:09:00.845 --> 00:09:01.678
and thoughts about infrastructure.

00:09:01.678 --> 00:09:02.705
I think--
- I've never met

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:05.025
anyone who's called themselves
an infrastructure nerd.

00:09:05.025 --> 00:09:05.957
So you get points just for that.

00:09:05.957 --> 00:09:06.790
- I've met a lot of them.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.205
- And you know someone else
who was an infrastructure.

00:09:08.205 --> 00:09:10.275
I'm probably an infrastructure nerd too.

00:09:10.275 --> 00:09:12.145
Now that I know that it's a thing.

00:09:12.145 --> 00:09:13.312
- Yeah, now that you know it's a thing,

00:09:13.312 --> 00:09:15.115
you are definitely an infrastructure nerd.

00:09:15.115 --> 00:09:16.755
It's impossible not to be, I think once

00:09:16.755 --> 00:09:18.785
you actually dig into it.

00:09:18.785 --> 00:09:21.105
Growing up in New York
city, you take the subways

00:09:21.105 --> 00:09:23.405
and when you started to ask questions,

00:09:23.405 --> 00:09:24.634
well, why is this train later?

00:09:24.634 --> 00:09:26.675
What's happening over here
and what what's going on

00:09:26.675 --> 00:09:27.775
with these tracks?

00:09:27.775 --> 00:09:29.955
It gets fascinating really quickly.

00:09:29.955 --> 00:09:32.115
And that's just the physical
infrastructure piece.

00:09:32.115 --> 00:09:34.385
And so at first we just started talking

00:09:34.385 --> 00:09:37.185
about how a foundation
like ours could be a part

00:09:37.185 --> 00:09:39.325
of the conversation about infrastructure.

00:09:39.325 --> 00:09:42.275
And as we were digging in
more and more on the topic,

00:09:42.275 --> 00:09:45.285
we had this work on digital infrastructure

00:09:45.285 --> 00:09:48.505
happening almost separately,
where we were thinking

00:09:48.505 --> 00:09:50.855
about things like the
open source community,

00:09:50.855 --> 00:09:53.921
open learning platforms,
which is where we put

00:09:53.921 --> 00:09:57.935
our Khan Academy relationship
in open learning.

00:09:57.935 --> 00:10:00.415
And started to see these intersections,

00:10:00.415 --> 00:10:03.265
the really obvious one is broadband access

00:10:03.265 --> 00:10:06.525
as sort of a physical
infrastructure asset.

00:10:06.525 --> 00:10:08.865
And your ability to access something

00:10:08.865 --> 00:10:11.105
like an open learning platform.

00:10:11.105 --> 00:10:14.155
And so we started to talk and
think just a little bigger

00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:17.865
and a little wider about what
infrastructure could mean.

00:10:17.865 --> 00:10:20.525
And that's what the first big aha was,

00:10:20.525 --> 00:10:23.375
it was that infrastructure
had to be more than

00:10:23.375 --> 00:10:27.246
just about the bridges, the
roads, that really this triangle

00:10:27.246 --> 00:10:30.665
of physical, digital and
social infrastructure.

00:10:30.665 --> 00:10:33.155
That's how we need to be
thinking about the assets

00:10:33.155 --> 00:10:34.595
that we should be investing in.

00:10:34.595 --> 00:10:36.285
The things that we should care about.

00:10:36.285 --> 00:10:40.525
The things that the government
should be safeguarding,

00:10:40.525 --> 00:10:44.324
our physical, digital, and
social assets that are underlying

00:10:44.324 --> 00:10:48.258
all of our society and
keeping us functional.

00:10:49.495 --> 00:10:50.328
- This is fascinating.

00:10:50.328 --> 00:10:51.615
And I wanna remind everyone watching,

00:10:51.615 --> 00:10:54.335
feel free to put questions
on YouTube and Facebook

00:10:54.335 --> 00:10:57.275
and our team will surface
it to myself and Katy

00:10:57.275 --> 00:10:59.905
and ask really about anything
about infrastructure,

00:10:59.905 --> 00:11:03.425
about endowments, about
... I'm going to ask Katy

00:11:03.425 --> 00:11:06.035
a little bit about how she even
found herself in this role.

00:11:06.035 --> 00:11:09.028
So really ask both me and
Katy anything y'all want,

00:11:09.028 --> 00:11:11.705
but just double clicking on that Katy,

00:11:11.705 --> 00:11:13.245
I think most of us are
familiar with physical

00:11:13.245 --> 00:11:16.725
infrastructure, those are
roads and bridges and dams

00:11:16.725 --> 00:11:19.135
and power lines and things like that.

00:11:19.135 --> 00:11:21.935
And I think we can imagine
digital infrastructure,

00:11:21.935 --> 00:11:24.075
which is related to
physical infrastructure,

00:11:24.075 --> 00:11:26.295
which is, I guess you could
debate whether it's physical

00:11:26.295 --> 00:11:30.305
or digital, but it's having
internet connectivity

00:11:30.305 --> 00:11:33.875
or at least having a device
or maybe having support

00:11:33.875 --> 00:11:35.095
for using that device.

00:11:35.095 --> 00:11:37.405
What is social infrastructure?

00:11:37.405 --> 00:11:40.835
- Yeah, so social infrastructure is really

00:11:40.835 --> 00:11:42.125
a little bit more intangible.

00:11:42.125 --> 00:11:44.885
You're thinking about the
networks that you're a part of,

00:11:44.885 --> 00:11:47.685
the relationships that you
have with other people.

00:11:47.685 --> 00:11:52.685
The one idea that I've talked
about with people before is,

00:11:53.295 --> 00:11:56.735
the notion that perhaps access
to childcare for example,

00:11:56.735 --> 00:12:00.125
is infrastructure because if
you're a parent as you are

00:12:00.125 --> 00:12:02.505
so you know that in order to work,

00:12:02.505 --> 00:12:05.165
you need someone to help
take care of your kids.

00:12:05.165 --> 00:12:08.725
And so how people are able
to contribute to the economy

00:12:08.725 --> 00:12:10.895
and be a part of that is really important

00:12:10.895 --> 00:12:13.335
to the nation's health and prosperity.

00:12:13.335 --> 00:12:16.455
So thinking about social
infrastructure as an element

00:12:16.455 --> 00:12:19.475
that matters, I think it
is a little bit trickier

00:12:19.475 --> 00:12:22.895
and can vary also depending on
the community that you're in.

00:12:22.895 --> 00:12:26.945
So, for some people having access to space

00:12:26.945 --> 00:12:31.435
where you can, coworking
space where entrepreneurship

00:12:31.435 --> 00:12:35.157
and a culture of
entrepreneurship is being built,

00:12:35.157 --> 00:12:39.645
can help them launch new
businesses and contribute

00:12:39.645 --> 00:12:40.875
to their local economy.

00:12:40.875 --> 00:12:44.545
We've invested in some work
in rural America related

00:12:44.545 --> 00:12:48.445
to helping create space and
cultures of entrepreneurship

00:12:48.445 --> 00:12:50.415
to kind of revive economies.

00:12:50.415 --> 00:12:53.125
So really it can vary.

00:12:53.125 --> 00:12:56.735
You can see in this
illustration that we talk

00:12:56.735 --> 00:13:00.245
about the digital economy,
we talk about libraries.

00:13:00.245 --> 00:13:02.245
Libraries are a place where
I think that physical,

00:13:02.245 --> 00:13:04.695
digital and social
infrastructure really intersect

00:13:04.695 --> 00:13:09.695
where you get both the access
to tech, the physical space,

00:13:10.655 --> 00:13:12.984
where you have information,
knowledge, and also

00:13:12.984 --> 00:13:16.555
it's opportunities to
take classes, to maybe

00:13:16.555 --> 00:13:20.405
learn a new language, to
access information about how

00:13:20.405 --> 00:13:22.885
to get online and how
to use new technology.

00:13:22.885 --> 00:13:25.045
All of that can happen at
a library, which was why

00:13:25.045 --> 00:13:27.215
they're one of our big
examples in the paper

00:13:27.215 --> 00:13:30.115
and a space for really exciting new ideas.

00:13:30.115 --> 00:13:33.295
I think, about what social infrastructure

00:13:33.295 --> 00:13:36.275
and what infrastructure in
communities can look like.

00:13:36.275 --> 00:13:38.105
- Now that you've named
social infrastructure.

00:13:38.105 --> 00:13:40.205
I now realize not only am
I an infrastructure nerd,

00:13:40.205 --> 00:13:42.445
I think I'm a social infrastructure nerd,

00:13:42.445 --> 00:13:45.945
because I spend a lot of
time thinking about maybe

00:13:45.945 --> 00:13:47.095
how do we social engineer?

00:13:47.095 --> 00:13:49.985
I mean, even in our direct
life, which obviously

00:13:49.985 --> 00:13:51.405
is more of a personal thing,

00:13:51.405 --> 00:13:53.305
like how do we make sure
we have deeper friendships?

00:13:53.305 --> 00:13:55.593
How do we make sure our kids
have places they can go to?

00:13:55.593 --> 00:13:58.325
How do you architect
that on a larger scale?

00:13:58.325 --> 00:14:00.165
Super, super fascinating.

00:14:00.165 --> 00:14:04.355
What are your big takeaways?

00:14:04.355 --> 00:14:06.055
Like what part of infrastructure

00:14:06.055 --> 00:14:07.995
are we doing well as a society?

00:14:07.995 --> 00:14:10.345
Are there examples of other
countries that are doing a

00:14:10.345 --> 00:14:14.358
better job where if y'all
were emperor of the universe,

00:14:14.358 --> 00:14:16.575
where would you invest?

00:14:16.575 --> 00:14:17.875
How would you invest that?

00:14:18.815 --> 00:14:23.705
- I think on many fronts,
we're not doing so well.

00:14:23.705 --> 00:14:25.811
So even on the physical
infrastructure front,

00:14:25.811 --> 00:14:29.505
we've seen, the American
society of civil engineers

00:14:29.505 --> 00:14:32.375
puts out a report card on how
our infrastructure is doing.

00:14:32.375 --> 00:14:36.400
They gave us a D plus,
which was not a great grade.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:40.165
And that's before you
consider these additional

00:14:40.165 --> 00:14:41.295
dimensions we have.

00:14:41.295 --> 00:14:44.255
That being said, in their bright spots,

00:14:44.255 --> 00:14:47.778
there are towns and cities
with access to fiber,

00:14:49.235 --> 00:14:50.695
high speed internet.

00:14:50.695 --> 00:14:53.085
There are places where the
library systems are doing

00:14:53.085 --> 00:14:56.695
an incredible job with reinforcing
the social infrastructure

00:14:56.695 --> 00:15:01.695
and coming into the new century
with these opportunities,

00:15:01.715 --> 00:15:04.552
for people to get online and
to learn how to do things that

00:15:04.552 --> 00:15:07.225
will help them get jobs and be a part

00:15:07.225 --> 00:15:08.775
of this new innovation economy.

00:15:09.745 --> 00:15:13.555
I think overall, we should
be thinking differently

00:15:13.555 --> 00:15:16.425
about how we spend the
dollars that we have

00:15:16.425 --> 00:15:20.775
to build new things and
to fix the old things.

00:15:20.775 --> 00:15:23.095
We haven't done a great
job of maintenance,

00:15:23.095 --> 00:15:25.375
ongoing maintenance, but
we should be investing

00:15:25.375 --> 00:15:27.740
with an eye toward how
any dollar that we spend

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.925
should have a payoff on all three fronts,

00:15:29.925 --> 00:15:31.105
not just one.

00:15:31.105 --> 00:15:33.075
And so I'm not going to
say that we shouldn't

00:15:33.075 --> 00:15:33.908
spend more money.

00:15:33.908 --> 00:15:35.255
I think we do need to spend more money.

00:15:35.255 --> 00:15:38.261
It happens at the state and
local and federal levels,

00:15:38.261 --> 00:15:41.132
but that we could also spend
the same amount of money

00:15:41.132 --> 00:15:43.225
and probably do a better job of it.

00:15:43.225 --> 00:15:45.613
If we looked at things and
thought about the payoff

00:15:45.613 --> 00:15:48.693
and also brought people to
the table who represented

00:15:48.693 --> 00:15:50.918
all three sides of the triangle.

00:15:51.915 --> 00:15:53.995
- Yeah, and to your point,
sometimes the same effort

00:15:53.995 --> 00:15:55.578
can actually hit all three sides.

00:15:55.578 --> 00:15:59.425
If it's thought out, what's
your sense of why we have un ...

00:15:59.425 --> 00:16:01.575
I mean, even just on
physical infrastructure,

00:16:01.575 --> 00:16:02.925
it feels like that's even like,

00:16:02.925 --> 00:16:04.890
it shouldn't be a political issue.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:09.452
I don't think anyone argues
with better roads, safe bridges,

00:16:09.452 --> 00:16:13.405
good utility infrastructure, et cetera.

00:16:13.405 --> 00:16:16.135
Cause you suffer, it's very
apparent when it's bad.

00:16:16.135 --> 00:16:18.095
It's very apparent when bad things happen

00:16:18.095 --> 00:16:20.891
or when power lines go
down and things like that.

00:16:20.891 --> 00:16:25.198
Why do we have this D grade right now?

00:16:26.375 --> 00:16:28.452
- I think it's a few
things, our infrastructure

00:16:28.452 --> 00:16:31.775
in the US especially
is super decentralized.

00:16:31.775 --> 00:16:35.105
So more spending, more
funding for infrastructure

00:16:35.105 --> 00:16:36.905
comes from the state and local level

00:16:36.905 --> 00:16:40.560
than the federal government,
even though people think about,

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.005
the federal government, when
you think about something

00:16:43.005 --> 00:16:46.005
as big as infrastructure,
but actually even

00:16:46.005 --> 00:16:48.908
in the physical sense most
highways are actually owned

00:16:48.908 --> 00:16:51.785
at the state level, not
at the federal level,

00:16:51.785 --> 00:16:54.875
even though lots of federal
investment went into creating

00:16:54.875 --> 00:16:58.258
the highway system and interstates
and that sort of thing.

00:16:59.405 --> 00:17:04.405
We are not necessarily
thinking about the future

00:17:06.245 --> 00:17:08.745
when we make infrastructure
investments either.

00:17:08.745 --> 00:17:12.705
So we are looking to fix
the immediate problems

00:17:12.705 --> 00:17:14.555
right in front of us,
but not thinking about

00:17:14.555 --> 00:17:16.303
the future uses and future needs.

00:17:16.303 --> 00:17:19.745
So by the time you can get
an infrastructure project

00:17:19.745 --> 00:17:23.265
through the bureaucracy, it
can often already be outdated

00:17:23.265 --> 00:17:25.945
before things even start being built.

00:17:25.945 --> 00:17:29.765
And I think anything that
involves spending, unfortunately,

00:17:29.765 --> 00:17:31.825
becomes a political issue, of course.

00:17:31.825 --> 00:17:34.035
And again, if we can
think differently about

00:17:34.035 --> 00:17:37.085
how to spend smartly, we might all,

00:17:37.085 --> 00:17:39.965
I think everyone loves a
deal, maybe deals and bargains

00:17:39.965 --> 00:17:41.305
are not necessarily political.

00:17:41.305 --> 00:17:43.101
So if we can make the case
that like you're getting

00:17:43.101 --> 00:17:46.125
a really good deal by
investing in infrastructure

00:17:46.125 --> 00:17:47.795
and looking at it through
these three lenses,

00:17:47.795 --> 00:17:50.805
then perhaps we could get
people to spend more money

00:17:50.805 --> 00:17:54.135
or invest differently without
it becoming a political issue.

00:17:54.135 --> 00:17:56.075
- Yeah, the argument with any investment,

00:17:56.075 --> 00:17:57.955
including infrastructures,
it should have some return.

00:17:57.955 --> 00:18:00.045
If you build a bridge, people
have a shorter commute time,

00:18:00.045 --> 00:18:02.380
it'll increase commerce,
and that will generate even,

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:04.355
not only more productivity,
which will generate

00:18:04.355 --> 00:18:06.555
more tax revenue, which you can use to pay

00:18:06.555 --> 00:18:08.195
for more infrastructure.

00:18:08.195 --> 00:18:09.855
There's a question here and it's related

00:18:09.855 --> 00:18:11.375
to what I just asked you.

00:18:11.375 --> 00:18:13.778
It's from YouTube, Hasset Gassess.

00:18:15.906 --> 00:18:18.105
Why do we think America's
infrastructure is worse

00:18:18.105 --> 00:18:20.095
than say Germany's
infrastructure, first of all,

00:18:20.095 --> 00:18:22.305
do you have a sense of
something like that true?

00:18:22.305 --> 00:18:24.775
And it feels like a
very comparable country.

00:18:24.775 --> 00:18:26.195
Is it based on how it's structured

00:18:26.195 --> 00:18:27.935
and the decentralized nature?

00:18:27.935 --> 00:18:30.175
Is it something is just,
we're different than Germans

00:18:30.175 --> 00:18:34.455
in how much we care about our
roads and bridges and things.

00:18:34.455 --> 00:18:36.365
- Yeah, I think there are
a lot of potential answers

00:18:36.365 --> 00:18:38.585
to that question, and I'm
sure that there are people

00:18:38.585 --> 00:18:40.725
who will agree and disagree
with my point of view on it.

00:18:40.725 --> 00:18:42.675
I think it's a combination
of all those things.

00:18:42.675 --> 00:18:47.675
The US is vast, even though
I think we have comparables

00:18:48.475 --> 00:18:52.665
in other countries like
Germany or even places in Asia

00:18:52.665 --> 00:18:55.595
like Japan or China where
they've done, I think a good job

00:18:55.595 --> 00:18:58.145
building infrastructure,
physical infrastructure.

00:18:58.145 --> 00:19:03.145
We also do have this really
different decentralized system.

00:19:03.895 --> 00:19:07.715
And we do have, I think, a
different approach in terms

00:19:07.715 --> 00:19:09.675
of how we think about whose responsibility

00:19:09.675 --> 00:19:12.135
is to take care of these
things and how quickly

00:19:12.135 --> 00:19:17.135
we're able to move and to gain
the partnership and create

00:19:18.045 --> 00:19:20.175
the opportunity for
new things to be built.

00:19:20.175 --> 00:19:23.325
I think we haven't made
it as much of a priority

00:19:23.325 --> 00:19:25.555
to invest in our infrastructure in the US

00:19:25.555 --> 00:19:26.888
as some other places have.

00:19:27.805 --> 00:19:28.725
- Hmm.

00:19:28.725 --> 00:19:31.565
And it's a related question from Facebook,

00:19:31.565 --> 00:19:34.725
Christabell Mendoza asks,
which cities in the US

00:19:34.725 --> 00:19:36.975
would be good models at
successfully encompass

00:19:36.975 --> 00:19:39.225
physical, social, and
digital infrastructure?

00:19:40.065 --> 00:19:41.232
- That's a really good question.

00:19:41.232 --> 00:19:44.065
And I'm hoping that as
part of what we're doing

00:19:44.065 --> 00:19:45.844
with talking about this
paper out in the world,

00:19:45.844 --> 00:19:48.605
that people will share more
examples with us of where

00:19:48.605 --> 00:19:50.955
they've seen really
great things happening,

00:19:50.955 --> 00:19:54.665
whether that's, in a city,
state, or even just a town

00:19:54.665 --> 00:19:56.855
think there are really
good examples at all levels

00:19:56.855 --> 00:20:00.185
and all sizes of how infrastructure,
of these three pieces

00:20:00.185 --> 00:20:03.785
of infrastructure can come
together, work better together.

00:20:03.785 --> 00:20:06.735
We have some good examples
in the white paper of places

00:20:08.397 --> 00:20:12.935
like Kansas city that have
really leveraged their access

00:20:12.935 --> 00:20:15.065
to fiber internet to
create an opportunities,

00:20:15.065 --> 00:20:16.795
that's coming back to
that libraries as example,

00:20:16.795 --> 00:20:18.315
we were talking about.

00:20:18.315 --> 00:20:21.235
Some of the towns that we work
with through our rural work.

00:20:21.235 --> 00:20:24.965
I'm not gonna name them because
there are now over a dozen

00:20:24.965 --> 00:20:27.955
and I don't wanna miss any,
but there are a few called out

00:20:27.955 --> 00:20:31.425
also in the paper, or if you
scroll through the website,

00:20:31.425 --> 00:20:35.225
which I think the link is
somewhere on the bottom

00:20:35.225 --> 00:20:38.604
of the screen, you can click
through some of the map

00:20:38.604 --> 00:20:41.695
that we have, which calls
out a few, a handful of towns

00:20:41.695 --> 00:20:44.405
where we've invested
that are doing a good job

00:20:44.405 --> 00:20:47.968
with this physical,
digital, social framework.

00:20:49.175 --> 00:20:51.405
- Are most of the examples,
you've given one example

00:20:51.405 --> 00:20:55.771
of fiber optic, high
bandwidth, that's both physical

00:20:55.771 --> 00:20:59.805
someone has to dig up
and lay down that fiber,

00:20:59.805 --> 00:21:02.095
but that's obviously digital
infrastructure as well.

00:21:02.095 --> 00:21:04.750
And then you make it available
in schools and libraries,

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:06.725
and then the community can benefit.

00:21:06.725 --> 00:21:08.875
And they're having kind
of a social connection,

00:21:08.875 --> 00:21:10.365
which is a social infrastructure.

00:21:10.365 --> 00:21:12.045
Are there other examples beyond,

00:21:12.045 --> 00:21:15.885
and there's a question here
from Suzanna Garcia Dominguez.

00:21:15.885 --> 00:21:18.276
Hi Katy, what is the
best optimization idea

00:21:18.276 --> 00:21:20.095
you have heard about infrastructure?

00:21:20.095 --> 00:21:22.485
Is it more of this
library flavor of things

00:21:22.485 --> 00:21:24.015
with high speed broadband?

00:21:24.015 --> 00:21:27.715
Are there other cool ideas you
all have been thinking about?

00:21:27.715 --> 00:21:30.565
- I think they're, well,
we'd love to hear cool ideas

00:21:30.565 --> 00:21:31.398
from all over.

00:21:31.398 --> 00:21:33.805
So there's a place both on the website,

00:21:33.805 --> 00:21:35.395
but also our emails are accessible.

00:21:35.395 --> 00:21:38.915
So we're always happy to
hear pie in the sky ideas

00:21:38.915 --> 00:21:40.275
for optimization here.

00:21:40.275 --> 00:21:44.535
I think a lot of what stands
out is related to this

00:21:44.535 --> 00:21:47.105
internet access, just
because, especially right now,

00:21:47.105 --> 00:21:49.565
especially in the
context of this pandemic,

00:21:49.565 --> 00:21:52.775
we've seen that be so important.

00:21:52.775 --> 00:21:56.805
And we've seen that there
still such an incredible divide

00:21:56.805 --> 00:21:59.855
in the country in terms of
who has strong internet access

00:21:59.855 --> 00:22:00.965
and who doesn't.

00:22:00.965 --> 00:22:03.565
And I think that's why
so many of the examples

00:22:03.565 --> 00:22:05.125
that are top of mind for me right now,

00:22:05.125 --> 00:22:09.392
and that feel, innovative and
important are related to that.

00:22:09.392 --> 00:22:12.705
But I think we're really
open to new ideas for how

00:22:12.705 --> 00:22:16.663
to optimize the using the
framework and want to experiment

00:22:16.663 --> 00:22:21.285
and find places that are
interested in just taking a chance

00:22:21.285 --> 00:22:23.688
on this idea and
implementing some new things.

00:22:25.512 --> 00:22:29.012
- I've been talking to a lot
of folks lately and I said,

00:22:29.012 --> 00:22:31.995
this COVID situation with
school closures is not good.

00:22:31.995 --> 00:22:34.150
It's a suboptimal, but if
there's a silver lining,

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.145
it's that, there's more energy than ever,

00:22:36.145 --> 00:22:37.835
in trying to close the digital divide.

00:22:37.835 --> 00:22:38.715
Are you all seeing that?

00:22:38.715 --> 00:22:40.065
And on a scale of one to 10,

00:22:40.065 --> 00:22:42.875
how hopeful are you that
in the next five years,

00:22:42.875 --> 00:22:45.222
the digital divide at
home, at least in the US

00:22:45.222 --> 00:22:47.358
is closed a good bit.

00:22:48.375 --> 00:22:50.415
- I'm really hopeful about that.

00:22:50.415 --> 00:22:55.415
I think we have seen not
just energy and enthusiasm

00:22:55.665 --> 00:22:57.835
around closing the digital
divide more than ever.

00:22:57.835 --> 00:23:00.403
We've also seen, I think, renewed interest

00:23:00.403 --> 00:23:03.865
from the private sector
in helping us get there,

00:23:03.865 --> 00:23:05.565
which is one of the things that
we talked about in the paper

00:23:05.565 --> 00:23:09.015
a bit too, is, we need to think
beyond just the government

00:23:09.015 --> 00:23:10.625
has money and should invest here.

00:23:10.625 --> 00:23:12.414
But thinking about how the private sector,

00:23:12.414 --> 00:23:15.255
how the social sector,
the philanthropic sector,

00:23:15.255 --> 00:23:17.905
non-profit world, that I'm a
part of, everyone contributes

00:23:17.905 --> 00:23:20.475
to infrastructure, both
in terms of the capital,

00:23:20.475 --> 00:23:22.923
the money that we're spending,
but also just the thoughts

00:23:22.923 --> 00:23:25.265
and the governance of infrastructure

00:23:25.265 --> 00:23:27.155
and how we shape it moving forward.

00:23:27.155 --> 00:23:30.365
So I think that is something
I'm super hopeful about,

00:23:30.365 --> 00:23:31.872
in terms of closing digital divide.

00:23:31.872 --> 00:23:34.145
I think we have a long way to go

00:23:34.145 --> 00:23:36.355
and we have to keep pushing,

00:23:36.355 --> 00:23:39.093
even if we get back to
a place where school's

00:23:39.093 --> 00:23:41.415
more in person than it has been,

00:23:41.415 --> 00:23:43.935
even if we're able to
work from offices again,

00:23:43.935 --> 00:23:45.735
this still matters.

00:23:45.735 --> 00:23:48.625
It's not just, if you
have to do school on zoom,

00:23:48.625 --> 00:23:50.775
that you should be worried
about the digital divide.

00:23:50.775 --> 00:23:52.905
It matters, if you're
trying to do homework

00:23:52.905 --> 00:23:55.915
at the end of the day, it
matters for all sorts of reasons.

00:23:55.915 --> 00:23:58.975
So I think if we can maintain this energy

00:23:58.975 --> 00:24:00.975
that we all have around
making sure that more people

00:24:00.975 --> 00:24:03.605
have access, I feel really
hopeful that we can solve

00:24:03.605 --> 00:24:06.605
this problem because we
actually know how to solve it.

00:24:06.605 --> 00:24:08.785
Like you bring the access to
the people, like you said,

00:24:08.785 --> 00:24:11.565
you dig the holes and
you lay the fiber cable,

00:24:11.565 --> 00:24:13.845
you do those things and
we can get access to folks

00:24:13.845 --> 00:24:15.225
who need it.

00:24:15.225 --> 00:24:17.345
- Yeah, and there's other
whole things I'm hopeful of,

00:24:17.345 --> 00:24:19.035
obviously the whole Starlink project,

00:24:19.035 --> 00:24:22.230
Elon Musk's micro satellites
that he's gonna pull out

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:23.999
that he's already putting into orbit,

00:24:23.999 --> 00:24:26.827
that'll give us internet
access almost anywhere.

00:24:26.827 --> 00:24:30.015
Yeah, good to hear that
you're a similarly hopeful

00:24:30.015 --> 00:24:32.185
because it does feel
like clean drinking water

00:24:32.185 --> 00:24:36.285
or just basic shelter
in the decades to come.

00:24:36.285 --> 00:24:39.305
Maybe in the remaining time,
I do always like to ask,

00:24:39.305 --> 00:24:41.165
we have sometimes a lot
of young people watching.

00:24:41.165 --> 00:24:44.125
And I remember when I was a young person,

00:24:44.125 --> 00:24:47.635
I would always wonder, how do
you ... you know of certain

00:24:47.635 --> 00:24:51.115
careers, doctors, lawyers,
engineers, and you can go

00:24:51.115 --> 00:24:55.016
to certain schools and
eventually end up being one

00:24:55.016 --> 00:24:58.615
of those professions, but
there's clearly many other jobs

00:24:58.615 --> 00:25:01.295
in the world that have
less clear career tracks,

00:25:01.295 --> 00:25:04.505
like being the executive
director of a family foundation.

00:25:04.505 --> 00:25:07.405
How did you, did you
see yourself doing this?

00:25:07.405 --> 00:25:08.565
What did you think you were going to do?

00:25:08.565 --> 00:25:11.557
And how did you end up in this role?

00:25:11.557 --> 00:25:13.115
- Yeah, it's sort of a funny story.

00:25:13.115 --> 00:25:15.725
And I'll try to tell you
the abridged version,

00:25:15.725 --> 00:25:20.305
but when I was in high
school and in college,

00:25:20.305 --> 00:25:22.085
in my sort of early years of college,

00:25:22.085 --> 00:25:24.515
I thought that I wanted to
work in the non-profit sector.

00:25:24.515 --> 00:25:26.215
I thought that I wanted
to start a non-profit.

00:25:26.215 --> 00:25:28.355
And then I learned that
there's too many non-profits

00:25:28.355 --> 00:25:29.685
and you probably don't
need to start a non-profit

00:25:29.685 --> 00:25:31.548
of your own, but maybe
you could go work in one.

00:25:31.548 --> 00:25:34.005
And then I learned about
philanthropy and I said,

00:25:34.005 --> 00:25:36.415
oh, well, better to sit on
the other side of the table,

00:25:36.415 --> 00:25:38.875
sounds good to give
away money for a living.

00:25:38.875 --> 00:25:41.996
And I really quickly
learned that there was not

00:25:41.996 --> 00:25:44.845
a clear career path into philanthropy

00:25:44.845 --> 00:25:47.555
that it's very bespoke.

00:25:47.555 --> 00:25:51.765
It depends a lot on the different
foundations individually,

00:25:51.765 --> 00:25:54.575
and that there are not a ton
of entry level positions.

00:25:54.575 --> 00:25:56.095
So I sort of forgot about it.

00:25:56.095 --> 00:25:57.705
I put it to the side and I said,

00:25:57.705 --> 00:25:59.358
maybe I'll get there one day.

00:26:00.275 --> 00:26:02.335
Until then I'll go back
to my idea of working

00:26:02.335 --> 00:26:03.935
in the non-profit sector.

00:26:03.935 --> 00:26:06.575
And I did work in a
non-profit, I went to school

00:26:06.575 --> 00:26:08.795
in Philadelphia and I
lived there for a few years

00:26:08.795 --> 00:26:09.965
after I graduated.

00:26:09.965 --> 00:26:12.995
I worked at a very cool
non-profit there that actually

00:26:12.995 --> 00:26:17.630
has a giant puppet park theater,
but also social activism,

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:21.685
non-profit called spiral Q and--

00:26:21.685 --> 00:26:24.248
- A giant puppet park theater.

00:26:25.287 --> 00:26:27.675
When you're just a giant
puppet, I was like,

00:26:27.675 --> 00:26:28.508
what more is that?

00:26:28.508 --> 00:26:30.205
But now I think I understand it.

00:26:30.205 --> 00:26:32.594
It's a giant puppet.
- (indistinct)

00:26:32.594 --> 00:26:36.955
They are invested in social activism

00:26:36.955 --> 00:26:39.885
and it started a long time ago as just,

00:26:39.885 --> 00:26:42.185
they would build these giant puppets

00:26:42.185 --> 00:26:43.795
and their protest puppets.

00:26:43.795 --> 00:26:47.075
And I can share a link later with anyone

00:26:47.075 --> 00:26:50.675
who's actually curious about
this, but it was a great time.

00:26:50.675 --> 00:26:52.245
And then there's little bit of luck.

00:26:52.245 --> 00:26:56.035
I happened to get a job at
Google when they were launching

00:26:56.035 --> 00:26:58.735
Google plus because they thought
there were some interesting

00:26:58.735 --> 00:27:00.765
use cases for the non-profit sector.

00:27:00.765 --> 00:27:02.395
And I had that experience.

00:27:02.395 --> 00:27:06.235
And then while I was at Google,
I took this winding path

00:27:06.235 --> 00:27:08.765
through doing many things
with the organization.

00:27:08.765 --> 00:27:11.625
One of those things was
corporate social responsibility,

00:27:11.625 --> 00:27:13.165
community engagement work.

00:27:13.165 --> 00:27:17.665
And that took me down a
interesting journey of learning

00:27:17.665 --> 00:27:20.525
what it was like to
connect with the community

00:27:20.525 --> 00:27:22.865
as a representative of the company.

00:27:22.865 --> 00:27:26.055
Got me some experience with
giving money away for a living.

00:27:26.055 --> 00:27:29.165
And that then took me to David's company.

00:27:29.165 --> 00:27:31.745
And then eventually
David and I just realized

00:27:31.745 --> 00:27:35.325
that we had things in common
and thought the same way.

00:27:35.325 --> 00:27:37.885
And I started to work with, what was then

00:27:37.885 --> 00:27:40.215
very small team of SFE.

00:27:40.215 --> 00:27:43.185
And it just so happened
that the timing was right.

00:27:43.185 --> 00:27:46.485
And I wound up taking a
job working just directly

00:27:46.485 --> 00:27:47.855
with David on the philanthropy.

00:27:47.855 --> 00:27:51.945
And just last year actually
became the executive director

00:27:51.945 --> 00:27:53.995
after we had some changes.

00:27:53.995 --> 00:27:57.155
So I think that's probably
not a helpful answer

00:27:57.155 --> 00:27:59.355
if you're trying to launch
a career in philanthropy.

00:27:59.355 --> 00:28:01.635
I will say two things, one
a little controversial,

00:28:01.635 --> 00:28:03.765
not going to graduate
school actually turned out

00:28:03.765 --> 00:28:06.460
to be really great for my career.

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.045
I may still go to graduate
school one day who knows,

00:28:09.045 --> 00:28:12.522
with this online world, I
may be able to swing it,

00:28:12.522 --> 00:28:14.225
but I always really wanted
to go to grad school,

00:28:14.225 --> 00:28:16.595
but could never decide
what I would focus on.

00:28:16.595 --> 00:28:18.555
Cause I'm just all over the place

00:28:18.555 --> 00:28:22.071
and think about different
things every hour.

00:28:22.071 --> 00:28:25.455
But not going to graduate
school and not focusing,

00:28:25.455 --> 00:28:27.725
made me a little more adaptable.

00:28:27.725 --> 00:28:30.415
And so I would pick up any
opportunity and look at it

00:28:30.415 --> 00:28:33.405
as a chance to learn something
and try it on for size.

00:28:33.405 --> 00:28:36.595
And that took me to a
really interesting place

00:28:36.595 --> 00:28:37.428
in my career.

00:28:37.428 --> 00:28:41.335
And the other thing is just,
I was lucky and timing played

00:28:41.335 --> 00:28:45.495
a big role and I think timing
and luck are what they are,

00:28:45.495 --> 00:28:48.385
but you have to, again, be
open to the opportunity,

00:28:48.385 --> 00:28:51.195
you have to be trying
to make the timing work.

00:28:51.195 --> 00:28:54.445
So consider anything that
comes your way as a chance

00:28:54.445 --> 00:28:57.982
to do something different
and see where it takes you.

00:28:57.982 --> 00:29:00.668
And you may end up running a foundation.

00:29:00.668 --> 00:29:03.165
- Yeah, I'm surprised that you say

00:29:03.165 --> 00:29:04.155
you like giving money away.

00:29:04.155 --> 00:29:07.341
I think asking for money is so much fun.

00:29:07.341 --> 00:29:09.135
(indistinct)

00:29:09.135 --> 00:29:10.002
It's actually fun.

00:29:10.002 --> 00:29:11.365
- I'm not very good at that either.

00:29:11.365 --> 00:29:13.855
I'm not great at asking for money.

00:29:13.855 --> 00:29:15.865
- No asking for money is not an, yeah,

00:29:15.865 --> 00:29:18.015
you gotta ruffle up your
hair, have your puppy,

00:29:18.015 --> 00:29:20.575
dog eyes and (laughs)

00:29:21.709 --> 00:29:23.285
- (indistinct) for those people though.

00:29:23.285 --> 00:29:25.435
Everyone should give
money to Khan Academy.

00:29:26.375 --> 00:29:27.785
- I agree with you, I agree.

00:29:27.785 --> 00:29:29.005
That's a really good point.

00:29:29.005 --> 00:29:30.018
I agree with you.

00:29:31.105 --> 00:29:33.005
Very high social return on investment.

00:29:33.985 --> 00:29:36.735
The last question, and
it's related to the one

00:29:36.735 --> 00:29:39.405
we just talked about from
YouTube Bashar Ostara says,

00:29:39.405 --> 00:29:40.655
what are some challenges you think

00:29:40.655 --> 00:29:41.755
the next generation will face?

00:29:41.755 --> 00:29:42.935
And I'll just broaden it.

00:29:42.935 --> 00:29:45.015
You're someone that is a young person.

00:29:45.015 --> 00:29:46.525
You're already thinking,
I wanna do something

00:29:46.525 --> 00:29:49.835
that has some social impact,
do good for the world.

00:29:49.835 --> 00:29:52.585
I think most young people
are in that same frame,

00:29:52.585 --> 00:29:53.805
but there's like, how do I do?

00:29:53.805 --> 00:29:56.245
What do you think are the,
any problem is an opportunity.

00:29:56.245 --> 00:29:57.525
So what do you think are some of the ones

00:29:57.525 --> 00:29:59.315
that they should start thinking about?

00:29:59.315 --> 00:30:03.295
- Yeah, I know that so many
young and younger people

00:30:03.295 --> 00:30:05.195
are thinking about this
already, but climate

00:30:05.195 --> 00:30:08.975
and climate change are so important for us

00:30:08.975 --> 00:30:09.825
to be thinking about.

00:30:09.825 --> 00:30:13.675
We, older people should
also be doing a better job

00:30:13.675 --> 00:30:16.425
tackling climate change,
but I think there's so much

00:30:16.425 --> 00:30:20.165
opportunity to anything from
inventing new technologies,

00:30:20.165 --> 00:30:22.475
to starting new non-profits
or social movements

00:30:22.475 --> 00:30:23.308
related to that.

00:30:23.308 --> 00:30:27.085
Like that work is so important and we have

00:30:27.085 --> 00:30:29.575
to solve these problems.

00:30:29.575 --> 00:30:31.315
There's no choice, but to solve them.

00:30:31.315 --> 00:30:33.635
So I think that's one
that I would call out.

00:30:33.635 --> 00:30:35.305
But coming back to some of the stuff

00:30:35.305 --> 00:30:36.355
we were talking about earlier,

00:30:36.355 --> 00:30:39.827
I think, thinking about how
tech can be used for good

00:30:39.827 --> 00:30:42.885
is not just a non-profit sector issue.

00:30:42.885 --> 00:30:46.355
It's an issue for anyone,
whether you're an academic,

00:30:46.355 --> 00:30:49.665
whether you're doing private sector work,

00:30:49.665 --> 00:30:52.105
no matter what, you can
be looking at the world

00:30:52.105 --> 00:30:54.802
and thinking how can
what I'm doing contribute

00:30:54.802 --> 00:30:58.525
to solving problems rather
than creating more problems?

00:30:58.525 --> 00:31:00.845
I think if we did more of that,

00:31:00.845 --> 00:31:04.268
no matter what we were doing,
we would all be better for it.

00:31:06.145 --> 00:31:07.475
- Well, that's great advice.

00:31:07.475 --> 00:31:09.785
And I can't imagine, yeah,
it's a very existential issue

00:31:09.785 --> 00:31:10.618
of climate change.

00:31:10.618 --> 00:31:13.425
So that's a good one to
highlight, but there's many more.

00:31:13.425 --> 00:31:15.015
What Katy, whenever we
have these conversations

00:31:15.015 --> 00:31:17.675
the time always go by
faster than I expect,

00:31:17.675 --> 00:31:19.525
but thank you so much for joining.

00:31:19.525 --> 00:31:20.921
This was a real treat.
- Yeah, thank you.

00:31:20.921 --> 00:31:22.545
- And thank you for your
support over the years.

00:31:22.545 --> 00:31:23.378
I'm not joking.

00:31:23.378 --> 00:31:25.105
It's been a big part of
what allows Khan Academy

00:31:25.105 --> 00:31:26.515
to be Khan Academy.

00:31:26.515 --> 00:31:27.348
- Well, thank you.

00:31:27.348 --> 00:31:28.545
Thank you for all that you do.

00:31:28.545 --> 00:31:31.565
I still watch Khan Academy
videos when I need to learn

00:31:31.565 --> 00:31:33.645
something new or refresh my memory.

00:31:33.645 --> 00:31:37.985
So appreciate your work
in more ways than one

00:31:37.985 --> 00:31:40.405
on a personal level and what
you're doing for the world.

00:31:40.405 --> 00:31:41.499
And this was fun.

00:31:41.499 --> 00:31:42.332
It's always fun to talk to you,

00:31:42.332 --> 00:31:44.968
but also great to talk also
to everyone else out there.

00:31:46.275 --> 00:31:47.155
- Yeah, last time we met,

00:31:47.155 --> 00:31:49.205
I remember that, this is all pre-COVID,

00:31:49.205 --> 00:31:50.038
where we could go to a restaurant.

00:31:50.038 --> 00:31:51.344
- To restaurants.

00:31:51.344 --> 00:31:54.185
- I remember there was
like some cheese fries

00:31:54.185 --> 00:31:58.175
that I was having like
self control issues with,

00:31:58.175 --> 00:31:59.008
but anyway, thank you--
- You had come out

00:31:59.008 --> 00:32:01.825
of a long trip you just needed sustenance.

00:32:01.825 --> 00:32:02.658
It happened to be french fries.

00:32:02.658 --> 00:32:07.155
- Yeah, my whole life is all
about discipline and like,

00:32:07.155 --> 00:32:10.191
but then the levy breaks and anyway,

00:32:10.191 --> 00:32:12.185
whole other conversation.

00:32:12.185 --> 00:32:13.018
Thanks Katy.

00:32:13.965 --> 00:32:14.798
- Thanks.

00:32:16.025 --> 00:32:17.355
- Well, thanks everyone for joining us,

00:32:17.355 --> 00:32:19.565
always a really fun conversation with Katy

00:32:19.565 --> 00:32:22.332
and I actually hope we can
get David Siegel on well

00:32:22.332 --> 00:32:26.094
from the Siegel Family Endowment
in the next several weeks.

00:32:26.094 --> 00:32:29.965
But thanks everyone for
joining our next homeroom,

00:32:29.965 --> 00:32:33.935
is gonna be tomorrow with Arne Duncan,

00:32:33.935 --> 00:32:36.745
secretary of education in
the Obama Administration.

00:32:36.745 --> 00:32:39.485
And he's a managing
partner at Chicago CRED.

00:32:39.485 --> 00:32:42.845
He does really incredible
work with young people,

00:32:42.845 --> 00:32:44.385
especially in Chicago.

00:32:44.385 --> 00:32:49.325
So Arne Duncan, who I also
consider to be a good friend

00:32:49.325 --> 00:32:50.905
and an all around good guy.

00:32:50.905 --> 00:32:53.355
So I look forward to that
conversation tomorrow.

00:32:53.355 --> 00:32:54.625
So thanks everyone for joining.

00:32:54.625 --> 00:32:58.187
And I'll see you tomorrow
with secretary Duncan.

